{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011SpdSCUNvb5NzzPf3N7nQr","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/binlog.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public surface of the binary trace (\"binlog\") backend.  The\n4\t//! header exposes two flavours of entry point:\n5\t//!\n6\t//! 1. **Top-level wiring** ([`Quic::set_binlog`],\n7\t//!    [`Quic::enable_binlog`]) that installs the binlog vtable on a\n8\t//!    QUIC context.  `binlog_dir == NULL` in C is the \"stop tracing\"\n9\t//!    sentinel; [`Quic::enable_binlog`] only flips the vtable\n10\t//!    pointer and is used when autoqlog wants the binary stream\n11\t//!    without a dedicated directory.\n12\t//! 2. **Per-event writers** that emit one trace record.  In C they\n13\t//!    all bottom out in `fwrite()` against either an out-of-band\n14\t//!    `FILE*` or `connection->f_binlog` pulled from the connection.  The\n15\t//!    Rust split mirrors that: the three file-only writers ([`pdu`],\n16\t//!    [`packet`], [`tls_ticket`]) are free functions on a [`File`]\n17\t//!    sink, and the rest hang on the [`Binlog`] trait implemented for\n18\t//!    [`Connection`] (they pull the file handle from\n19\t//!    `connection.f_binlog` themselves).\n20\t//!\n21\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n22\t//! Bodies and the empty-test module land in later phases.\n23\t//!\n24\t//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes for this module:\n25\t//!\n26\t//! * `FILE*` (the three low-level writers) → `&mut std::fs::File`.\n27\t//!   These calls borrow the handle for the duration of one record\n28\t//!   write — ownership stays with the connection (`connection->f_binlog`)\n29\t//!   or the caller.  The binlog stream is *binary*, so the text-side\n30\t//!   `&mut impl core::fmt::Write` convention used by\n31\t//!   [`crate::textlog`] does not apply here.\n32\t//! * `Quic*` / `Connection*` — every observed caller passes a non-NULL\n33\t//!   handle and the body mutates internal state (`quic->bin_log_fns`,\n34\t//!   `connection->f_binlog`, `quic->binlog_dir`).  Free functions\n35\t//!   keyed on `Quic*` become inherent methods on [`Quic`]; those keyed\n36\t//!   on `Connection*` become methods on the [`Binlog`] trait\n37\t//!   (implemented for [`Connection`]).\n38\t//! * `Path*` — only ever accessed inside\n39\t//!   `binlog_get_path_id(connection, path_x)`, which dereferences `path_x`\n40\t//!   to read `unique_path_id`.  Every observed caller passes a\n41\t//!   non-NULL path handle, so this is `&mut Path` for parity with\n42\t//!   the unified-log dispatch trait (which takes the path mutably\n43\t//!   for the same hooks).\n44\t//! * `const ConnectionId*` (in [`pdu`] / [`packet`]) →\n45\t//!   `&ConnectionId`.  The C contract is \"must be non-NULL\"; every\n46\t//!   caller passes `&connection->initial_connection_id`.\n47\t//! * `ConnectionId* dcid` (in [`Binlog::packet_lost`]) is\n48\t//!   nullable per `loss_recovery.c` — when no remote CID is known\n49\t//!   the C call site passes NULL and the body emits a single zero\n50\t//!   length byte.  Maps to `Option<&ConnectionId>`.  The C signature\n51\t//!   drops `const` but the body only reads through the pointer, so\n52\t//!   the Rust equivalent stays a shared borrow.\n53\t//! * `packet_header* ph` (in [`Binlog::dropped_packet`],\n54\t//!   [`Binlog::outgoing_packet`]) — the body only *reads*\n55\t//!   `ph->ptype` in the dropped path and reconstructs a fresh header\n56\t//!   in the outgoing path.  In [`Binlog::outgoing_packet`] the\n57\t//!   parsed-header buffer is constructed locally, so no pointer\n58\t//!   crosses the API boundary.  In [`packet`] /\n59\t//!   [`Binlog::dropped_packet`] we map `ph` to `&PacketHeader`\n60\t//!   (immutable borrow) — matching the unified-log trait shape.\n61\t//! * `ConnectionId connection_id` (in [`tls_ticket`]) is `Copy` and\n62\t//!   pass-by-value, mirroring the C ABI.\n63\t//! * `const struct sockaddr*` pairs (`addr_peer`, `addr_local` in\n64\t//!   [`pdu`]) → `&core::net::SocketAddr`, matching the convention\n65\t//!   established in [`crate::logger`].\n66\t//! * `const uint8_t* + size_t` argument pairs collapse to `&[u8]`\n67\t//!   (`bytes`/`bytes_max` in [`packet`], `params`/`param_length` in\n68\t//!   [`Binlog::transport_extension`], `ticket`/`ticket_length`\n69\t//!   in [`tls_ticket`], `sni`/`sni_len` and `alpn`/`alpn_len` in\n70\t//!   [`Binlog::negotiated_alpn`]).  An empty slice models the C\n71\t//!   `(NULL, 0)` callers cleanly.\n72\t//! * [`Binlog::outgoing_packet`] carries *two* buffers: the\n73\t//!   unencrypted `bytes` (length passed separately as `length`) and\n74\t//!   the encrypted `send_buffer` (length passed separately as\n75\t//!   `send_length`).  Each pair collapses to one `&[u8]`.  The\n76\t//!   `pn_length` parameter — the offset of the packet-number field\n77\t//!   inside `bytes` — survives as a separate `usize`.\n78\t//! * `const PtlsIovec* alpn_list, size_t alpn_count` →\n79\t//!   `&[&[u8]]`, mirroring the unified-log trait shape.\n80\t//! * `int receiving` / `int is_local` are pure 0/1 flags promoted to\n81\t//!   `bool`; `int err` is a real signed status code so it stays\n82\t//!   `i32`.\n83\t//! * `unsigned char ecn` collapses to `u8`.\n84\t//! * `char const* binlog_dir` is the C \"stop tracing when NULL\"\n85\t//!   sentinel, so [`Quic::set_binlog`] takes `Option<&str>`.\n86\t//!   `Some(path)` installs the vtable and copies the directory name\n87\t//!   into the QUIC context; `None` leaves the directory cleared\n88\t//!   while still wiring up the vtable.\n89\t//! * `char const* trigger` (in [`Binlog::packet_lost`]) is\n90\t//!   always a non-NULL static string literal at the call sites\n91\t//!   grepped in `loss_recovery.c`, so it maps to `&str`.\n92\t//!\n93\t//! Error handling: [`Quic::set_binlog`] mirrors the C `int` return\n94\t//! (always `0` today, but reserved for future failure modes) as\n95\t//! `Result<(), Error>` per the project's error-handling convention.\n96\t\n97\tuse std::fs::File;\n98\tuse std::path::Path as FsPath;\n99\t\n100\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n101\t\n102\tuse crate::Error;\n103\tuse crate::Instant;\n104\tuse crate::internal::{Connection, PacketHeader, PacketType, Path};\n105\tuse crate::{ConnectionId, Quic};\n106\t\n107\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n108\t// Event-tag enum.\n109\t//\n110\t// The C `picoquic_log_event_type` is a sparse enum with explicit\n111\t// hex constants — the values are baked into the binary log format\n112\t// and must round-trip through the wire untouched.  Translated as a\n113\t// Rust enum with `#[repr(u32)]` so each variant keeps its\n114\t// wire-format tag; `repr(C)` is *not* used because the type does\n115\t// not cross an FFI boundary — the discriminant is only serialized\n116\t// as a varint by `binlog_compose_event_header`.\n117\t\n118\t/// Event tag emitted at the start of every binary log record.  The\n119\t/// underlying integer values are part of the binlog wire format and\n120\t/// must not drift from the C enum.\n121\t#[repr(u32)]\n122\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n123\tpub enum LogEventType {\n124\t    PduSent = 0x0002,\n125\t    PduRecv = 0x0003,\n126\t\n127\t    PacketSent = 0x0008,\n128\t    PacketRecv = 0x0009,\n129\t\n130\t    NewConnection = 0x0010,\n131\t    ConnectionClose = 0x0011,\n132\t    ConnectionIdUpdate = 0x0012,\n133\t    PacketLost = 0x0013,\n134\t    PacketDropped = 0x0014,\n135\t    PacketBuffered = 0x0015,\n136\t\n137\t    TlsKeyUpdate = 0x0020,\n138\t    TlsKeyRetired = 0x0021,\n139\t\n140\t    VersionUpdate = 0x0035,\n141\t    ParamUpdate = 0x0036,\n142\t    AlpnUpdate = 0x0037,\n143\t    CcUpdate = 0x0038,\n144\t    StreamUpdate = 0x0039,\n145\t    InfoMessage = 0x003a,\n146\t\n147\t    FrameSent = 0x0082,\n148\t    FrameRecv = 0x0083,\n149\t}\n150\t\n151\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n152\t// Low-level per-event writers.\n153\t//\n154\t// Free functions on a [`File`] sink — these don't carry a `Connection`\n155\t// handle, so calls are namespaced through the module path\n156\t// (`binlog::pdu(...)`, `binlog::packet(...)`,\n157\t// `binlog::tls_ticket(...)`).  Callers usually go through the\n158\t// [`Connection`] methods below, which thread through `connection.f_binlog`; the\n159\t// file-only writers are kept public for the contexts where the\n160\t// caller already owns the handle (e.g., the binlog backend's\n161\t// implementation of [`crate::logger::Logger`]).\n162\t\n163\t/// Write a PDU arrival/departure record to `f`.\n164\t///\n165\t/// C: `void binlog_pdu(FILE*, const ConnectionId*, int,\n166\t/// uint64_t, const struct sockaddr*, const struct sockaddr*, size_t,\n167\t/// uint64_t, unsigned char)`.\n168\tpub fn pdu(\n169\t    _f: &mut File,\n170\t    _cid: &ConnectionId,\n171\t    _receiving: bool,\n172\t    _current_time: Instant,\n173\t    _addr_peer: &SocketAddr,\n174\t    _addr_local: &SocketAddr,\n175\t    _packet_length: usize,\n176\t    _unique_path_id: u64,\n177\t    _ecn: u8,\n178\t) {\n179\t    todo!()\n180\t}\n181\t\n182\t/// Write a decrypted-packet record to `f`.  Binary alternative to\n183\t/// `log_decrypted_segment()`.\n184\t///\n185\t/// C: `void binlog_packet(FILE*, const ConnectionId*,\n186\t/// uint64_t, int, uint64_t, const packet_header*,\n187\t/// const uint8_t*, size_t)`.\n188\tpub fn packet(\n189\t    _f: &mut File,\n190\t    _cid: &ConnectionId,\n191\t    _path_id: u64,\n192\t    _receiving: bool,\n193\t    _current_time: Instant,\n194\t    _ph: &PacketHeader,\n195\t    _bytes: &[u8],\n196\t) {\n197\t    todo!()\n198\t}\n199\t\n200\t/// Write a TLS session-ticket record to `f`.  Binary alternative\n201\t/// to `log_tls_ticket()`.  The connection-id parameter is `Copy`\n202\t/// and passed by value to mirror the C ABI.\n203\t///\n204\t/// C: `void binlog_picotls_ticket(FILE*, ConnectionId,\n205\t/// uint8_t*, uint16_t)`.\n206\tpub fn tls_ticket(_f: &mut File, _cnx_id: ConnectionId, _ticket: &[u8]) {\n207\t    todo!()\n208\t}\n209\t\n210\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n211\t// High-level per-event writers — methods on [`Connection`].\n212\t//\n213\t// Each method pulls the file handle from `connection.f_binlog` and\n214\t// delegates to one of the low-level writers above (or composes\n215\t// several records).  Bundling them as a [`Binlog`] trait (rather\n216\t// than inherent methods on `Connection`) lets callers opt into the\n217\t// capability with `use crate::binlog::Binlog`, scopes the names to\n218\t// the trait, and keeps the names short — no `binlog_` prefix\n219\t// needed since the trait disambiguates.\n220\t\n221\t/// Binary trace recording for a [`Connection`].  Each method writes\n222\t/// one record (or composes a few records) into the connection's\n223\t/// `f_binlog` file; bringing the trait into scope opts the caller\n224\t/// into the capability.  C: the per-event writers in\n225\t/// `quic/binlog.c`.\n226\tpub trait Binlog {\n227\t    /// Report that a packet was dropped due to some error.\n228\t    ///\n229\t    /// C: `void binlog_dropped_packet(Connection*, Path*,\n230\t    /// packet_header*, size_t, int, uint64_t)`.\n231\t    fn dropped_packet(\n232\t        &mut self,\n233\t        path_x: &mut Path,\n234\t        ph: &PacketHeader,\n235\t        packet_size: usize,\n236\t        err: i32,\n237\t        current_time: Instant,\n238\t    );\n239\t\n240\t    /// Report that a packet was buffered waiting for decryption.\n241\t    ///\n242\t    /// C: `void binlog_buffered_packet(Connection*, Path*,\n243\t    /// packet_type_enum, uint64_t)`.\n244\t    fn buffered_packet(&mut self, path_x: &mut Path, ptype: PacketType, current_time: Instant);\n245\t\n246\t    /// Binary alternative to `log_outgoing_segment()`.  `bytes`\n247\t    /// is the unencrypted payload (the C `length` parameter is\n248\t    /// folded into the slice); `send_buffer` is the encrypted,\n249\t    /// padded wire form.  `pn_length` is the offset of the\n250\t    /// packet-number field inside `bytes`.\n251\t    ///\n252\t    /// C: `void binlog_outgoing_packet(Connection*, Path*,\n253\t    /// uint8_t*, uint64_t, size_t, size_t, uint8_t*, size_t,\n254\t    /// uint64_t)`.\n255\t    fn outgoing_packet(\n256\t        &mut self,\n257\t        path_x: &mut Path,\n258\t        bytes: &[u8],\n259\t        sequence_number: u64,\n260\t        pn_length: usize,\n261\t        send_buffer: &[u8],\n262\t        current_time: Instant,\n263\t    );\n264\t\n265\t    /// Log a packet-lost event.  `dcid` is `None` when the remote\n266\t    /// connection ID is unknown — the C body emits a single zero\n267\t    /// byte in that case.\n268\t    ///\n269\t    /// C: `void binlog_packet_lost(Connection*, Path*,\n270\t    /// packet_type_enum, uint64_t, char const*,\n271\t    /// ConnectionId*, size_t, uint64_t)`.\n272\t    fn packet_lost(\n273\t        &mut self,\n274\t        path_x: &mut Path,\n275\t        ptype: PacketType,\n276\t        sequence_number: u64,\n277\t        trigger: &str,\n278\t        dcid: Option<&ConnectionId>,\n279\t        packet_size: usize,\n280\t        current_time: Instant,\n281\t    );\n282\t\n283\t    /// Log negotiated SNI / ALPN.  Empty `sni`/`alpn` slices stand\n284\t    /// in for the C `(NULL, 0)` callers.\n285\t    ///\n286\t    /// C: `void binlog_negotiated_alpn(Connection*, int,\n287\t    /// uint8_t const*, size_t, uint8_t const*, size_t,\n288\t    /// const PtlsIovec*, size_t)`.\n289\t    fn negotiated_alpn(&mut self, is_local: bool, sni: &[u8], alpn: &[u8], alpn_list: &[&[u8]]);\n290\t\n291\t    /// Binary alternative to `log_transport_extension()`.\n292\t    ///\n293\t    /// C: `void binlog_transport_extension(Connection*, int, size_t,\n294\t    /// uint8_t*)`.\n295\t    fn transport_extension(&mut self, is_local: bool, params: &[u8]);\n296\t\n297\t    /// Open the per-connection binlog file and emit the\n298\t    /// `new_connection` record.  Idempotent — the C body is a no-op\n299\t    /// when neither `quic->binlog_dir` nor `quic->qlog_dir` are\n300\t    /// set, or when `quic->bin_log_fns` is `NULL`.\n301\t    ///\n302\t    /// C: `void binlog_new_connection(Connection*)`.\n303\t    fn new_connection(&mut self);\n304\t\n305\t    /// Emit the `connection_close` record and close the\n306\t    /// per-connection binlog file.  Safe to call when no binlog is\n307\t    /// currently open (the C body guards on `connection->f_binlog !=\n308\t    /// NULL`).\n309\t    ///\n310\t    /// C: `void binlog_close_connection(Connection*)`.\n311\t    fn close_connection(&mut self);\n312\t\n313\t    /// Log the state of the congestion controller, retransmission\n314\t    /// queues, etc.  Called either just after processing an\n315\t    /// incoming packet or just after sending one.\n316\t    ///\n317\t    /// C: `void binlog_cc_dump(Connection*, Path*, uint64_t)`.\n318\t    fn cc_dump(&mut self, path_x: &mut Path, current_time: Instant);\n319\t}\n320\t\n321\timpl Binlog for Connection {\n322\t    fn dropped_packet(\n323\t        &mut self,\n324\t        _path_x: &mut Path,\n325\t        _ph: &PacketHeader,\n326\t        _packet_size: usize,\n327\t        _err: i32,\n328\t        _current_time: Instant,\n329\t    ) {\n330\t        todo!()\n331\t    }\n332\t\n333\t    fn buffered_packet(&mut self, _path_x: &mut Path, _ptype: PacketType, _current_time: Instant) {\n334\t        todo!()\n335\t    }\n336\t\n337\t    fn outgoing_packet(\n338\t        &mut self,\n339\t        _path_x: &mut Path,\n340\t        _bytes: &[u8],\n341\t        _sequence_number: u64,\n342\t        _pn_length: usize,\n343\t        _send_buffer: &[u8],\n344\t        _current_time: Instant,\n345\t    ) {\n346\t        todo!()\n347\t    }\n348\t\n349\t    fn packet_lost(\n350\t        &mut self,\n351\t        _path_x: &mut Path,\n352\t        _ptype: PacketType,\n353\t        _sequence_number: u64,\n354\t        _trigger: &str,\n355\t        _dcid: Option<&ConnectionId>,\n356\t        _packet_size: usize,\n357\t        _current_time: Instant,\n358\t    ) {\n359\t        todo!()\n360\t    }\n361\t\n362\t    fn negotiated_alpn(\n363\t        &mut self,\n364\t        _is_local: bool,\n365\t        _sni: &[u8],\n366\t        _alpn: &[u8],\n367\t        _alpn_list: &[&[u8]],\n368\t    ) {\n369\t        todo!()\n370\t    }\n371\t\n372\t    fn transport_extension(&mut self, _is_local: bool, _params: &[u8]) {\n373\t        todo!()\n374\t    }\n375\t\n376\t    fn new_connection(&mut self) {\n377\t        todo!()\n378\t    }\n379\t\n380\t    fn close_connection(&mut self) {\n381\t        todo!()\n382\t    }\n383\t\n384\t    fn cc_dump(&mut self, _path_x: &mut Path, _current_time: Instant) {\n385\t        todo!()\n386\t    }\n387\t}\n388\t\n389\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n390\t// Top-level wiring on the QUIC context.\n391\t\n392\timpl Quic {\n393\t    /// Set the binary-log directory and install the binlog vtable\n394\t    /// on this context.  Pass `None` to clear the directory while\n395\t    /// still leaving the vtable in place — that is the C \"stop\n396\t    /// binary tracing\" sentinel (`binlog_dir == NULL`).\n397\t    ///\n398\t    /// C: `int picoquic_set_binlog(picoquic_quic_t*, char const*)`\n399\t    /// — the return is `0` today but reserved for failure modes;\n400\t    /// mapped to `Result<(), Error>` per the project's\n401\t    /// error-handling convention.\n402\t    pub fn set_binlog(\n403\t        &mut self,\n404\t        _binlog_dir: Option<&(impl AsRef<FsPath> + ?Sized)>,\n405\t    ) -> Result<(), Error> {\n406\t        todo!()\n407\t    }\n408\t\n409\t    /// Enable binary logging without setting a directory — used\n410\t    /// when autoqlog wants the binlog stream as scratch space.\n411\t    /// Equivalent to [`Quic::set_binlog`] minus the directory\n412\t    /// bookkeeping.\n413\t    ///\n414\t    /// C: `void picoquic_enable_binlog(picoquic_quic_t*)`.\n415\t    pub fn enable_binlog(&mut self) {\n416\t        todo!()\n417\t    }\n418\t}\n419\t\n420\t#[cfg(test)]\n421\tmod test {}\n422\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4dc870de-e938-4c75-83bb-2bc85323ae97","uuid":"a4871bc0-6f7e-45ec-9b6d-5a48e8391c6d","timestamp":"2026-05-05T05:26:01.753Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/binlog.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/binlog.h`.\n//!\n//! Public surface of the binary trace (\"binlog\") backend.  The\n//! header exposes two flavours of entry point:\n//!\n//! 1. **Top-level wiring** ([`Quic::set_binlog`],\n//!    [`Quic::enable_binlog`]) that installs the binlog vtable on a\n//!    QUIC context.  `binlog_dir == NULL` in C is the \"stop tracing\"\n//!    sentinel; [`Quic::enable_binlog`] only flips the vtable\n//!    pointer and is used when autoqlog wants the binary stream\n//!    without a dedicated directory.\n//! 2. **Per-event writers** that emit one trace record.  In C they\n//!    all bottom out in `fwrite()` against either an out-of-band\n//!    `FILE*` or `connection->f_binlog` pulled from the connection.  The\n//!    Rust split mirrors that: the three file-only writers ([`pdu`],\n//!    [`packet`], [`tls_ticket`]) are free functions on a [`File`]\n//!    sink, and the rest hang on the [`Binlog`] trait implemented for\n//!    [`Connection`] (they pull the file handle from\n//!    `connection.f_binlog` themselves).\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n//! Bodies and the empty-test module land in later phases.\n//!\n//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes for this module:\n//!\n//! * `FILE*` (the three low-level writers) → `&mut std::fs::File`.\n//!   These calls borrow the handle for the duration of one record\n//!   write — ownership stays with the connection (`connection->f_binlog`)\n//!   or the caller.  The binlog stream is *binary*